 new definition that\n               re-implements what a sibling already does is the drift\n               this command exists to prevent.\n            3. Widen the job before you write any of it. Read this\n               scenario's neighbours in `docs/scenarios.yaml` — the\n               scenarios of the same `service:` that follow it, about\n               ten of them — and collect THEIR steps that nothing under\n               `<suite path>/steps/` binds either. You are authoring a\n               vocabulary, not patching one scenario: every such step\n               you leave unbound buys another whole validate-fix-apply\n               cycle when the walk reaches it, and the walk reaches it\n               within minutes.\n            4. Register one definition per unbound step you collected —\n               this scenario's and the neighbours':\n               `suite.Step(`^<anchored regexp>$`, <func>)`. Put a\n               capture group where the step's text varies — a quoted\n               run, a number — so one definition serves every scenario\n               phrasing it that way, and do not write a definition whose\n               pattern is a literal copy of one scenario's line.\n            5. Implement every function against the suite's state type.\n               Given prepares, When acts, Then asserts and returns an\n               error naming what it expected and what it got. A\n               neighbour's definition is held to the same standard as\n               this scenario's — an unimplemented stub is not a fix.\n            6. Do not weaken an existing definition to make a step\n               match. Do not edit the registry.\n            7. Never write a definition for a step whose text opens with\n               `llm:` or `judge:`. Those are run by a model by the\n               scenario author's explicit choice, and a pattern that\n               captured one would take that choice back.\n","numLines":104,"startLine":1,"totalLines":104