gister` function — do not add a second registration function.\\n- Do not relax any pattern, do not add a definition for a step opening\\n  with `llm:` or `judge:` (this suite has none), and do not modify\\n  `docs/scenarios.yaml` or any file outside `tests/mcp/steps/`.\\n- Do not create `tests/mcp/scenarios/` or edit the generated\\n  `tests/mcp/mcp_test.go`; they are outside this fix's write root.\\n\\n## Verification after applying\\n\\n`tests/mcp/steps/` must now bind, by anchored pattern:\\n\\n- `the MCP server is running` → `^the MCP server is running$`\\n- `the Claude User opens a session` → `^the (.+) opens a session$`\\n- `the session is granted a ticket` → `^the session is granted a ticket$`\\n- `the server names its protocol version` → `^the server names its protocol version$` (INT-902)\\n\\nVerdict for INT-901 and INT-902 alike: executable, 0 steps unbound.\\n\",\"questions\":[]}","structured_output":{"fix_prompt":"# Fix Prompt for Scenario INT-901: A Claude User opening a session at the MCP server is granted a ticket\n\n## Target\n\nSuite resolved from `docs/architecture/architecture.yaml`: the scenario's\n`service:` is `mcp-service`, and the only `tests/<service>/` tree is\n`tests/mcp` (the architecture's `testing.commands` drive `./tests/mcp/...`).\nThe suite's steps package is therefore:\n\n    tests/mcp/steps/\n\nIt does **not exist yet** — `tests/mcp/` holds only the generated\n`mcp_test.go`. So there is no sibling state type to reuse: this fix\nestablishes the suite's state type, its `Register` function and the whole\nstep vocabulary of the suite in one pass.\n\nWrite **only** under `tests/mcp/steps/`. Do not touch\n`docs/scenarios.yaml`, `docs/product/`, `docs/architecture/`, `true-bdd/`,\nor `tests/mcp/mcp_test.go`.\n\n## Vocabulary this fix must bind (subject + neighbour, one pass)\n\nFour definitions, covering every unbound step of INT-901 **and** INT-902:\n\n| pattern | block | catalogue phrase |\n|---|---|---|\n| `^the MCP server is running$` | given | `the MCP server is running` |\n| `^the (.+) opens a session$` | when | `the {actor:role} opens a session` (capture group stands in for `{actor:role}`) |\n| `^the session is granted a ticket$` | then | `the session is granted a ticket` |\n| `^the server names its protocol version$` | then | `the server names its protocol version` (INT-902's unbound Then) |\n\nEach observes its words exactly as the architecture's word mapping says:\naddress from `MCP_PORT` (7411 when unset) on 127.0.0.1 polled at\n`GET /healthz` until 200 for at most 5s; the session opened by one\n`POST /mcp` of `{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"method\":\"initialize\"}` with\n`Content-Type: application/json`, whose status and headers are kept; the\nticket read from `X-Session-Ticket` (32 lowercase hex, on a 200 reply);\nthe protocol version read from `X-MCP-Protocol` (a YYYY-MM-DD date). A\nThen step that finds no kept reply fails rather than passes.\n\nOnly the role in the When step varies, so only it gets a capture group —\nthe other three phrases carry no placeholder, and their patterns are\nanchored literals of the *catalogue phrase*, not of a scenario line.\n\n## Required Changes\n\n### Change #1: the suite's shared state and its observation helpers\n\n**File:** `tests/mcp/steps/state.go`\n**Action:** Use the **Write** tool (new file).\n**Content:**\n```go\n// Package steps binds the registry steps of the `mcp` suite to code.\n//\n// The suite's state is the MCP server address the Given step resolved\n// and the reply to the session's opening the When step kept; every Then\n// step reads that reply.\npackage steps\n\nimport (\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"io\"\n\t\"net/http\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"time\"\n)\n\nconst (\n\t// portEnv and defaultPort are the architecture's address mapping:\n\t// 127.0.0.1 at MCP_PORT, 7411 when it is unset.\n\tportEnv     = \"MCP_PORT\"\n\tdefaultPort = \"7411\"\n\tlocalHost   = \"127.0.0.1\"\n)\n\nconst (\n\thealthTimeout       = 5 * time.Second\n\thealthPollInterval  = 50 * time.Millisecond\n\thealthReqTimeout    = time.Second\n\topenSessionTimeout  = 10 * time.Second\n\tinitializeRequest   = `{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"method\":\"initialize\"}`\n)\n\n// Reply is what a Then step may read of the session's opening: the\n// status code and the headers of the server's reply to initialize.\ntype Reply struct {\n\tStatus int\n\tHeader http.Header\n}\n\n// State is the state the suite's step definitions share.\ntype State struct {\n\taddr  string\n\treply *Reply\n}\n\n// New returns the state one scenario's steps run against.\nfunc New() *State {\n\treturn &State{}\n}\n\n// Addr is the MCP server address the Given step resolved.\nfunc (s *State) Addr() string {\n\treturn s.addr\n}\n\n// openedSession returns the reply the When step kept. A Then step that\n// finds none fails instead of passing.\nfunc (s *State) openedSession() (*Reply, error) {\n\tif s.reply == nil {\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\n\t\t\t\"no session was opened: expected the When step to POST initialize to %s/mcp and keep the reply, but no reply was kept\",\n\t\t\ts.serverAddrForMessage(),\n\t\t)\n\t}\n\n\treturn s.reply, nil\n}\n\nfunc (s *State) serverAddrForMessage() string {\n\tif s.addr == \"\" {\n\t\treturn \"(no server address resolved)\"\n\t}\n\n\treturn s.addr\n}\n\n// serverAddr resolves the MCP server's address from MCP_PORT, 7411 when\n// it is unset.\nfunc serverAddr() string {\n\tport := os.Getenv(portEnv)\n\tif port == \"\" {\n\t\tport = defaultPort\n\t}\n\n\treturn \"http://\" + localHost + \":\" + port\n}\n\n// waitHealthy polls GET /healthz until it answers 200, for at most\n// healthTimeout, and names the address it polled when it never does.\nfunc waitHealthy(addr string) error {\n\tctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), healthTimeout)\n\tdefer cancel()\n\n\tclient := &http.Client{Timeout: healthReqTimeout}\n\tlast := \"no attempt completed\"\n\n\tfor ctx.Err() == nil {\n\t\tstatus, err := getHealth(ctx, client, addr)\n\t\tif err == nil && status == http.StatusOK {\n\t\t\treturn nil\n\t\t}\n\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\tlast = err.Error()\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tlast = fmt.Sprintf(\"status %d\", status)\n\t\t}\n\n\t\ttime.Sleep(healthPollInterval)\n\t}\n\n\treturn fmt.Errorf(\n\t\t\"the MCP server is not running: expected GET %s/healthz to answer 200 within %s, last attempt gave %s\",\n\t\taddr, healthTimeout, last,\n\t)\n}\n\nfunc getHealth(ctx context.Context, client *http.Client, addr string) (int, error) {\n\treq, err := http.NewRequestWithContext(ctx, http.MethodGet, addr+\"/healthz\", nil)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn 0, fmt.Errorf(\"building GET %s/healthz: %w\", addr, err)\n\t}\n\n\tresp, err := client.Do(req)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn 0, fmt.Errorf(\"GET %s/healthz failed: %w\", addr, err)\n\t}\n\tdefer func() { _ = resp.Body.Close() }()\n\n\t_, _ = io.Copy(io.Discard, resp.Body)\n\n\treturn resp.StatusCode, nil\n}\n\n// openSession POSTs the initialize request to /mcp and keeps the reply's\n// status code and headers.\nfunc openSession(addr string) (*Reply, error) {\n\tctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), openSessionTimeout)\n\tdefer cancel()\n\n\treq, err := http.NewRequestWithContext(\n\t\tctx, http.MethodPost, addr+\"/mcp\", strings.NewReader(initializeRequest),\n\t)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"building POST %s/mcp: %w\", addr, err)\n\t}\n\n\treq.Header.Set(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json\")\n\n\tresp, err := http.DefaultClient.Do(req)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"POST %s/mcp with %s failed: %w\", addr, initializeRequest, err)\n\t}\n\tdefer func() { _ = resp.Body.Close() }()\n\n\t_, _ = io.Copy(io.Discard, resp.Body)\n\n\treturn &Reply{Status: resp.StatusCode, Header: resp.Header.Clone()}, nil\n}\n```\n\n### Change #2: the suite's `Register` function and its four step definitions\n\n**File:** `tests/mcp/steps/steps.go`\n**Action:** Use the **Write** tool (new file).\n**Content:**\n```go\npackage steps\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"net/http\"\n\t\"regexp\"\n\t\"time\"\n)\n\nconst (\n\tticketHeader   = \"X-Session-Ticket\"\n\tprotocolHeader = \"X-MCP-Protocol\"\n\n\t// ticketPattern and protocolLayout are the architecture's shapes for\n\t// the two headers of the session's opening.\n\tticketPattern  = `^[0-9a-f]{32}$`\n\tprotocolLayout = \"2006-01-02\"\n)\n\n// Suite is what a step definition is registered with: the runner, which\n// matches a step's text and calls the definition with the pattern's\n// capture groups.\ntype Suite interface {\n\tStep(pattern string, fn func(state *State, args []string) error)\n}\n\n// Register binds every step of the `mcp` suite; one call site lists\n// everything the suite binds.\nfunc Register(suite Suite) {\n\tsuite.Step(`^the MCP server is running$`, serverIsRunning)\n\tsuite.Step(`^the (.+) opens a session$`, opensSession)\n\tsuite.Step(`^the session is granted a ticket$`, sessionIsGrantedTicket)\n\tsuite.Step(`^the server names its protocol version$`, serverNamesProtocolVersion)\n}\n\n// serverIsRunning resolves the server's address and waits until it\n// answers GET /healthz with 200.\nfunc serverIsRunning(state *State, _ []string) error {\n\taddr := serverAddr()\n\tif err := waitHealthy(addr); err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\n\tstate.addr = addr\n\tstate.reply = nil\n\n\treturn nil\n}\n\n// opensSession asks the MCP server for a new session once, and keeps the\n// reply for the steps that follow.\nfunc opensSession(state *State, args []string) error {\n\tif len(args) != 1 {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"expected the step pattern to capture the role, got %d capture group(s)\", len(args))\n\t}\n\n\trole := args[0]\n\n\tif state.addr == \"\" {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\n\t\t\t\"the %s cannot open a session: no MCP server address was resolved, so the Given step %q must run first\",\n\t\t\trole, \"the MCP server is running\",\n\t\t)\n\t}\n\n\tif state.reply != nil {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\n\t\t\t\"the %s already opened a session (reply status %d): this step opens exactly one session\",\n\t\t\trole, state.reply.Status,\n\t\t)\n\t}\n\n\treply, err := openSession(state.addr)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"the %s could not open a session: %w\", role, err)\n\t}\n\n\tstate.reply = reply\n\n\treturn nil\n}\n\n// sessionIsGrantedTicket asserts the reply to initialize carries a\n// ticket: 32 lowercase hexadecimal characters, on a 200.\nfunc sessionIsGrantedTicket(state *State, _ []string) error {\n\treply, err := state.openedSession()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\n\tticket := reply.Header.Get(ticketHeader)\n\n\tif reply.Status != http.StatusOK {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\n\t\t\t\"the session was not granted a ticket: expected status 200 with a %s header of 32 lowercase hexadecimal characters, got status %d with %s: %q\",\n\t\t\tticketHeader, reply.Status, ticketHeader, ticket,\n\t\t)\n\t}\n\n\tif !regexp.MustCompile(ticketPattern).MatchString(ticket) {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\n\t\t\t\"the session was not granted a ticket: expected the %s header of the 200 reply to be 32 lowercase hexadecimal characters, got %q\",\n\t\t\tticketHeader, ticket,\n\t\t)\n\t}\n\n\treturn nil\n}\n\n// serverNamesProtocolVersion asserts the reply to initialize names the\n// protocol version as a YYYY-MM-DD date.\nfunc serverNamesProtocolVersion(state *State, _ []string) error {\n\treply, err := state.openedSession()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\n\tversion := reply.Header.Get(protocolHeader)\n\tif _, perr := time.Parse(protocolLayout, version); perr != nil {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\n\t\t\t\"the server did not name its protocol version: expected the %s header of the session's opening to be a date in YYYY-MM-DD form, got %q (reply status %d)\",\n\t\t\tprotocolHeader, version, reply.Status,\n\t\t)\n\t}\n\n\treturn nil\n}\n```\n\n## Constraints on applying this\n\n- Create both files with **Write**; they are new. If `tests/mcp/steps/`\n  already holds Go files from an earlier p